[Rule]
padded-blocks
[Overview]
Require or disallow padding within blocks


Some style guides require block statements to start and end with blank lines. The goal is
to improve readability by visually separating the block content and the surrounding code.


```json
if (a) {

    b();

}
```

Since it’s good to have a consistent code style, you should either always write
padded blocks or never do it.
[Details]
This rule enforces consistent empty line padding within blocks.
[Options]
The ESLint rule "padded-blocks" has several configurable options. Here are the details for each option:

### First Option (String or Object)
